<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oaths of Strasbourg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/842">842 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oaths of Strasbourg were mutual pledges of alliance sworn in 842 CE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, grandsons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, against their brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>. They were recorded in early Romance and Germanic vernaculars so that each army could understand them. They are the earliest surviving texts in recognisable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a>, an important legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg.jpg" alt="Oaths of Strasbourg" width="210" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renardeau.arctique · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serments_de_Strasbourg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) divided the empire into kingdoms that foreshadowed France and Germany; the <b>Oaths of Strasbourg</b> (842 CE), sworn in both a Romance vernacular and Old High German, are the earliest surviving text in a language recognisable as early Old French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Fontenoy in 841 Louis and Charles defeated Lothar, and in 842 they confirmed their alliance with the <b>Strasbourg Oaths</b>, sworn in early forms of Romance and German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Oaths of Strasbourg</b> of 842, sworn by two of Charlemagne’s grandsons, are the earliest surviving text in an early form of French.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li></ul></section>
